lued as central to democratic citizenship.", "quotes": [{"text": "These findings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based explanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered central to democratic citizenship.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Engaged but hostile users are not confined to fringe audiences. They post more and have more friends and followers, which may give them greater reach.", "quotes": [{"text": "Finally, the results show that engaged but hostile social media users do not simply cater to fringe audiences; they post more frequently and have more friends and followers.", "page": 1}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The data link YouGov panel surveys of American users with their scraped public X/Twitter posts: 2,567 linked panelists and 9,250,180 posts, collected from 2019 to 2021.", "quotes": [{"text": "We rely on a data infrastructure combining panel survey data with online behavioral data from X (see also [59]).", "page": 6}, {"text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period was 2567.", "page": 6}, {"text": "During this period, we managed to scrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists.", "page": 6}]}, {"claim": "The primary hostility measure is a word-embedding 'Super-Unsupervised' score. Each post is scored by cosine similarity to a combined 'political'+'hate' vector, and scores are averaged per user.", "quotes": [{"text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word embeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach [60].", "page": 6}, {"text": "we create a measure of a respondent's general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts", "page": 7}]}, {"claim": "The measure was validated against expert hand-labeled posts. It reached 74% accuracy on a held-out test set.", "quotes