lity, rather than replacing those accounts.", "quotes": [{"text": "In this article, we examine a different possibility.", "page": 2}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "The classical political-behavior literature (e.g., Verba, Schlozman and Brady; Nie et al.) treats political engagement as a boon to democracy, equipping citizens for respectful conversation. The authors dispute this by showing engagement is associated with hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Within the classical literature, political engagement has been seen as a boon to democracy.", "page": 4}, {"text": "Formal education and a strong sense of internal efficacy potentially equip citizens with the motivation and capacity to partake in respectful conversations about topics of political importance online.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Almond and Verba argued that engagement must be tempered and can go too far. The authors build on this classical caveat.", "quotes": [{"text": "In particular [20], argued that engagement needed to be tempered with a more parochial relationship to politics", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "The motivated-reasoning literature (e.g., Leeper and Slothuus; Taber and Lodge) finds that engagement and political knowledge exacerbate biased processing. The authors build on this.", "quotes": [{"text": "this literature also provided evidence that biased information processing is further exacerbated among those high in political knowledge [48,49].", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Grievance-based accounts tie hostility to deprivation and loss of status. The authors argue hostility is also linked to engagement, not only to these factors.", "quotes": [{"text": "From this perspective, hostility may be connected to experiences of deprivation, exclusion, or perceived loss of social standing among certain segments of the population", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "Psychological research on cyberbullying and trolling attributes the most severe hate to a small segment of users with extreme personality traits. The aut